#{[17592186662822 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{x}=⟨1,3,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨0,-1,k⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨2,5,1⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [17592186663434 "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-1,1,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨4,1,6⟩.$"] [17592186666149 "![](images/a4/a43e4291dcbe0819d5c320b21805c992f32789123622ab01e7d407a45540defc.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ and $-4\\mathbf{a}?$"] [17592186707146 "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - 3 y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 4$ where $x\\ge0,\\ y\\ge0$."] [17592186707148 "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D x + 2 y\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 9$ where $x\\le0,\\ y\\le0$."] [17592186707149 "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 4$ where $x\\ge0,\\ y\\le0$."] [17592186707163 "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D \\frac{3}{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 9$ where $x\\le0,\\ y\\le0$."] [17592186707164 "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 1$ where $y\\le0$."] [17592186707194 "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - \\frac{y}{x}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the part of the disk where $y\\ge0$ bounded by $x^2+y^2=- 3 x$, shown below.\n\n![](images/eb/eb6ab6771940a63f6c826f7fef79305115b2f83f894e54c6d60f038982000833.png)"] [17592186707196 "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D \\frac{2 y}{x}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the part of the disk where $y\\ge0$ bounded by $x^2+y^2=- 2 x$, shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6c8c5af2bfe7fe3579510bceabdfdb9c40ed413eaba6a3aaaf9e90c30eb9b62.png)"] [17592186707199 "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D 3 x \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the part of the disk where $x\\le0$ bounded by $x^2+y^2=3 y$, shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3ff147b4f3ab0dc7db522a812b6d8d7505506a28b5dc31d33a562184240c721e.png)"] [17592186707203 "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 1$."] [17592186708439 "![](images/60/6087da076e65bda5240c428ff67c570ad59ae9a8e5a5a3c9e8406184998de56a.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ is the line segment from $O(0,0)$ to $A(1,0)$, and $C_2$ is the line segment from $A(1,0)$ to $B(0,-2)$. Traverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in that order.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x+y)\\mathbf i+(2x-2y)\\mathbf j$. Given that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\frac{11}{2},\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n.\n\nFor $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r_1(t)=t\\mathbf i+0\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le1$."] [17592186708440 "![](images/87/87182d625f4e669a6b2791a17f3e12c34922043b8f3deefd6b2473e8fb529886.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ is the line segment from $O(0,0)$ to $A(2,0)$, and $C_2$ is the section of the parabola $y=4-x^2$ from $A(2,0)$ to $B(0,4)$. Traverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in that order.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x+y)\\mathbf i+2x\\mathbf j$. Given that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=2,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n."] [17592186708445 "![](images/04/0457cb819e6f39ef9c0978e356cff788e1cf5edbef9cbf844a054ab777b70eac.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ is the line segment from $A(-2,0)$ to $B(0,1)$, and $C_2$ is the line segment from $B(0,1)$ to $D(1,1)$. Traverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in that order.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x-2y)\\mathbf i+(2x+y)\\mathbf j$. Given that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\frac32,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n."] [17592186708455 "![](images/06/06f90fbcd495493bcbab5bd9aa0109e89a6ac092aabb6949c3385bce4d543cb1.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of $C_1$, $C_2$, and $C_3$. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. For the same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$, you are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=6,\\\\\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3,\\\\\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the integral along $C_2$ in the direction of its arrows.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}"] [17592186708457 "![](images/3e/3e0e8cf7cb700cb6d9ee074a0bb3eac1c82b24229b8d66e636121e53998fee7e.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the five segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used for every integral. Let $L_1$ follow $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$, and let $L_2$ follow $A\\to D\\to C\\to A$. You are given\n\n$$\n\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=3,\\qquad \\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-5.\n$$\n\nThe closed path $C$ follows the **outer boundary** $A\\to B\\to C\\to D\\to A$, counterclockwise. Evaluate its line integral.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}"] [17592186708672 "![Directed segments: C_1: D to C; C_2: B to C; C_3: D to A; C_4: B to A; C_5: A to C](images/54/5480c83b384f2abd235cdb9b380bd5a75f15a0de271692c288b5c93b340231da.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. Let $L_1$ follow $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ and $L_2$ follow $A\\to D\\to C\\to A$. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=5\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to D\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. This path is the **outer boundary**. Evaluate its line integral.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}"] [17592186708674 "![Directed segments: C_1: D to A; C_2: A to B; C_3: B to C; C_4: D to C; C_5: C to A](images/a1/a14ec792c3924723ca962f3921cd836bc3ddf012f4485bb447da2ca8cae10f15.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. Let $L_1$ follow $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ and $L_2$ follow $A\\to C\\to D\\to A$. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe closed path $C$ follows $A\\to D\\to C\\to B\\to A$ **clockwise**. This path is the **outer boundary**. Evaluate its line integral.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}"] [17592186708679 "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: A to D; C_3: C to D; C_4: B to C; C_5: A to C](images/f3/f336318d756a33f21dba0dba2bd52aaff23d8b266cec2c4f031bf7ef695dd080.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. Let $L_1$ follow $A\\to C\\to B\\to A$ and $L_2$ follow $A\\to C\\to D\\to A$. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=4\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to D\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. This path is the **outer boundary**. Evaluate its line integral.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}"] [17592186708680 "![Directed segments: C_1: C to B; C_2: D to A; C_3: C to D; C_4: C to A; C_5: B to A](images/ca/ca44141368bac2cbba7ced4f762c7502e41fa94dff30ce4eebf4876603feb33e.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. Let $L_1$ follow $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ and $L_2$ follow $A\\to C\\to D\\to A$. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-1\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to D\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. This path is the **outer boundary**. Evaluate its line integral.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}"] [17592186708684 "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: C to B; C_3: A to C](images/aa/aa0c4824cd83fc9db89a280bc804427adabf51610a1d62b39d1a7292ac16bb2e.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=6\\\\\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=6\\\\\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the integral along $C_3$ in the direction of its arrows.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}"] [17592186708685 "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: B to C; C_3: C to A](images/38/38a4b8304ca0a8122168f6e01824a13e899daaeb9b1b71e5f0320323d5ee78c1.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=0\\\\\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=0\\\\\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the integral along $C_2$ in the direction of its arrows.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}"] [17592186708686 "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: C to B; C_3: C to A](images/df/dff3182806adf9689792a2a8f3d048f0734637628aed2bd0622c382218821306.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to C\\to B\\to A$ **clockwise**. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=6\\\\\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\\\\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the integral along $C_1$ in the direction of its arrows.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}"] [17592186708694 "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: C to B; C_3: C to A](images/df/dff3182806adf9689792a2a8f3d048f0734637628aed2bd0622c382218821306.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=14\\\\\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=4\\\\\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the integral along $C_2$ in the direction of its arrows.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}"] [17592186708938 "![](images/0e/0e4a78829ce8d1135d46dda1db59c968c0306937d9c5a45b24fa41648a679ea4.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(2 x)\\mathbf i+(- x + 2 y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=4,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n."] [17592186708940 "![](images/60/6087da076e65bda5240c428ff67c570ad59ae9a8e5a5a3c9e8406184998de56a.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(1,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,-2).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(2 x - 2 y)\\mathbf i+(- x)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=1,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n."] [17592186708956 "![](images/04/0457cb819e6f39ef9c0978e356cff788e1cf5edbef9cbf844a054ab777b70eac.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of the line segment from the point $B(0,1)$ to the point $D(1,1).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x + 2 y)\\mathbf i+(- y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\frac{5}{2},\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n."] [17592186708960 "![](images/73/73c6a8268a179f72611fb65e099d1249d48501e4fd4731d03747800e1944fd97.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,2)$ to the point $D(1,3).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(0)\\mathbf i+(- 2 x + y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n."] [17592186708961 "![](images/93/93c6184f39b28a057866bb02e11e5e81a7abea4ff8a9a907670dd30cfc93d557.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,1)$ to the point $O(0,0).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(y)\\mathbf i+(- x - 2 y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-2,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n."] [17592186708976 "![](images/c0/c0a4f042748ddadd824bed4ee71bca9724d7ca7dcc376a0ccf7c1353d59ec4fc.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,2)$ to the point $B(-1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(-1,2)$ to the point $D(-1,0).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- x + y)\\mathbf i+(- x - y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n.\n\nFor $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r_1(t)=(t - 2)\\mathbf i+(2)\\mathbf j$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$."] [17592186708980 "![](images/0e/0e4a78829ce8d1135d46dda1db59c968c0306937d9c5a45b24fa41648a679ea4.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 2 x - y)\\mathbf i+(- y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=3,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n.\n\nFor $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r_1(t)=(2 t)\\mathbf i+(0)\\mathbf j$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$."] [17592186708993 "![](images/73/73c6a8268a179f72611fb65e099d1249d48501e4fd4731d03747800e1944fd97.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,2)$ to the point $D(1,3).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 2 x)\\mathbf i+(x + y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=1,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n.\n\nFor $C_2$, use $\\mathbf r_2(t)=(1)\\mathbf i+(t + 2)\\mathbf j$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$."] [17592186708998 "![](images/c0/c0a4f042748ddadd824bed4ee71bca9724d7ca7dcc376a0ccf7c1353d59ec4fc.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,2)$ to the point $B(-1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(-1,2)$ to the point $D(-1,0).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- x - y)\\mathbf i+(2 x)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=- \\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n.\n\nFor $C_2$, use $\\mathbf r_2(t)=(-1)\\mathbf i+(2 - 2 t)\\mathbf j$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$."] [17592186712210 "Evaluate\n$$\n\\int_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r,\n$$\nwhere $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=x\\,\\mathbf i+z\\,\\mathbf j+y\\,\\mathbf k$ and\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+2t\\,\\mathbf j+(1-t)\\,\\mathbf k,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n$$\n*Hint: Use\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^1\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\mathrm dt\n$$ with $\\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf j-\\mathbf k$.*"] [17592186712367 "Evaluate\n\n$$\n{\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(2 y - 1)\\,i+(2 x - y + 1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(- 2 t - 2)\\,i+(- t - 1)\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,1].$\n\n*Hint:* Use\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm d r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt\n$$."] [17592186712374 "Evaluate\n\n$$\n{\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- y)\\,i+(- x - 2)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t + 2)\\,i+(2 t + 1)\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,1].$\n\n*Hint:* Use\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm d r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt\n$$."] [17592186712378 "Evaluate\n\n$$\n{\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 y)\\,i+(- 2 x)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t - 2)\\,i+(2 t + 2)\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,1].$\n\n*Hint:* Use\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm d r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt\n$$."] [17592186712387 "Evaluate\n\n$$\n{\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- y + 1)\\,i+(- x + 1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(- t)\\,i+(- t - 1)\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,1].$\n\n*Hint:* Us